{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And the days of Terah were, etc.</b> The reason it did not say in the order stated for all the generations, ״וַיִּהְיוּ כָּל יְמֵי״ (\"and all the days of… were\"), has two possible meanings: either to say that he lived more than his allotted days, or that he did not complete his years. And here both interpretations are sustainable: that he lived more than the years allotted to him, as reward for what he did and exerted on behalf of Abram as stated below — he lived longer. And the proof is that you will find that Nahor, father of Terah, lived 148 years, and Terah exceeded him by 57 years; and behold, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 65) that when a person reaches the years of his two ancestors he should worry five years before or five years after, yet Terah lived many more years than his father. Therefore Scripture juxtaposed the lifespan of Terah to his departure with Abram, to inform you who caused him these long days — it was by virtue of ״וַיֵּצְאוּ אִתָּם״ etc. (\"and they went out with them,\" etc.). And accordingly, had it said ״כָּל יְמֵי תֶרַח״ (\"all the days of Terah\"), it would have been understood that these were the years allotted to him from the supernal world; but that is not so — rather, he merited and they added to him." "<b>A second approach</b> is that it is possible that more years were allotted to him than he lived, had he fulfilled his intention to go to the land of Canaan; and the reason he did not live his years in full is because he went out with Abram to go to the land of Canaan but did not fulfill his vow, and came only as far as Haran and settled there — therefore he did not complete his years. And therefore Scripture informed us of this matter, that they set out to go to the land of Canaan and came as far as Haran and settled there — this is the reason that the days of Terah were two hundred and five years." "<b>And I have seen fit</b> to open the eye to one detail concerning Abraham: how he left his father, who had gone out with him on his account, and went on his way. For you will find that Terah was 143 years old when Abraham our father separated from him, since he was 70 years old when Abraham was born, and when Abraham went to the land of Canaan, Abraham was 75 years old — at that point Terah's years add up to 143 — and he died at 205 years of age. And it is not fitting for Abraham to leave Terah his father, who had gone out for his sake, and go on his way. And even though HaShem said to him ״לֶךְ לְךָ״ (\"go for yourself\"), they already said that HaShem said to him ״לֶךְ לְךָ״ — according to your own desire to go, for He agreed with him; you see, then, that Abraham himself wanted to go." "<b>And it appears</b> that Abraham's reasoning was this: he thought in his mind that his father's days were complete when he reached the age of 143, and HaShem had added five years upon them, as they of blessed memory said — that one should worry five years before the lifespan of his father; behold, 148, which is the life of Nahor. And at that time he left him and went on his way. And HaShem agreed with him and said to him ״לֶךְ לְךָ.\" And even though he lived longer, nevertheless, since he did not merit the remaining years except in Abraham's merit, Abraham is not obligated to delay on their account. And perhaps this is what HaShem hinted at in saying <b>לֶךְ לְךָ</b> — meaning: ״לֵךְ״ (\"go\") and do not be concerned about the remaining years, because ״לְךָ״ (\"they are yours\") — meaning: they are from yours, i.e., from your merit. And there are many midrashim on this matter, and the plain sense is not to be explained away." "<b>Thus concludes the portion of Noah.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:37.691408995Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}